WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:09.240
SPEAKER 1: At first glance,
Google Cloud Platform is huge.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.990
There are so many tools,
services, and APIs

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:14.420
that sometimes it can be
difficult to know how it all

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.369
fits together.

00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:17.660
SPEAKER 2: Especially if you
have existing applications

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.780
where what you use and
what GCP offers don't

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.100
seem to match up directly.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:23.558
SPEAKER 1: If you've
been intrigued

00:00:23.558 --> 00:00:25.310
by the prospect of
migrating to GCP--

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.070
SPEAKER 2: --but haven't known
where to start, don't worry.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.520
SPEAKER 1: We are here to help.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:32.061
SPEAKER 2: Let's take a look at
a couple of application types

00:00:32.061 --> 00:00:33.440
and see how GCP lines up.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.960
We'll start simple
with static websites.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.800
No fancy service stuff here.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.710
Just some raw HTML
and JavaScript

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.900
files for client
side processing.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.970
For this example,
Google Cloud Storage

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:46.400
is the perfect place to start.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.940
GCS offers global object
storage and fetching via URL,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:51.920
so content can be
fetched directly

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.260
without having to
invoke any server code.

00:00:54.260 --> 00:00:56.300
Uploads are available
almost immediately,

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.360
replicated and backed up
worldwide on Google's network

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:00.680
and infrastructure.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.120
With partnerships with
CDNs around the world,

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.780
GCS can also reduce
the cost of and speed

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:07.820
to access your content.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.170
And the best part?

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.910
You can create a
domain that points

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.640
right to your GCS
buckets, so to users it

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.590
looks like a regular website.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.150
They don't need to
worry themselves

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:20.510
about the whole infinitely
scalable CDN thing.

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.760
So far so good.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.570
SPEAKER 1: But
also pretty simple.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.360
Next, let's look at something
a little bit more complex,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.910
but still very common--

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.890
virtual private servers.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:33.470
These are VMs running
somewhere in the cloud.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.110
You might be running a
game server or a chat bot

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:39.230
or anything else that needs
a dedicated machine and root

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.220
access.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:43.160
Google Compute Engine can
do the same thing for you,

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.230
but comes with a lot
more bells and whistles.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:49.080
GCE is a full infrastructure
as a service offering,

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.680
including a set of
Linux and Window images

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.280
and the ability to configure
your VMs with the exact CPU

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.590
and RAM specifications
that you need.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:01:59.570
It's also got a lot
of other handy stuff

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:01.280
that you might not
find elsewhere,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.650
like live migration,
multiple region

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.630
support, local SSDs, instant
snapshots, and preemptible VMs.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.520
Not to mention
production level things

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.180
like auto scaling, health check
systems, and full integration

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.800
into Stackdriver--
which provides

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:19.470
tracing, error handling, and
monitoring on your behalf.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.650
So if you're running
a private server

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:24.110
but don't want to deal with
all the overhead of managing

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.410
a private server, GCE
is a great option.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:29.060
But in reality,
there's a good chance

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:31.670
that you have an application
which is a mix of the two.

00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.620
You serve some static
content but also

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.540
have server side compute needs.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.970
In that case, doing all the
heavy lifting of managing

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.250
dedicated VMs-- and
paying for them--

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.620
might be overkill.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.230
SPEAKER 2: Which is exactly
what the platform-as-a-service

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:46.460
offerings are for.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.680
App Engine Standard lets
you serve static content

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.030
and also provides a server
side compute to interact with.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.350
We handle all the deployment,
migration, regions,

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.420
and scaling, and
you get to focus

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.050
on getting your app to users.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.410
GAE Standard has a
flexible billing model

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:03.540
and allows your app to scale
down to zero instances.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.510
So you don't pay if
no one's using it.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.390
Plus it has built-in access
to other GCP services

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:12.050
like Datastore, Cloud SQL,
Memcache, Bquery, Auth

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.160
and more.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:14.900
The trade-off for
all these benefits

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:18.270
is that App Engine Standard
comes with a few restrictions.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.330
There's a fixed set
of supported languages

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:22.820
and you don't get access to
the disk of the instance.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.650
And you definitely
don't have root access.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.960
But if you are just
deploying a client server

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.720
system with minimal
headache, that's

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.460
an easy trade-off to make.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:33.680
If you do need more
control but still want

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:36.080
all the managed
goodness, App Engine Flex

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.580
is worth investigating.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:39.920
Flex gives you all the same
management, auto scaling,

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:41.490
and bandwidth
splitting as Standard,

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.270
but also lets you run
with full root privileges

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.610
with any language or
container you want.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.380
The trade-off here
is that Flex doesn't

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.690
allow you to scale to zero
and has a different pricing

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:51.590
structure.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:53.780
So you'll definitely want
to make sure to check out

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.520
the pricing calculator.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.980
SPEAKER 1: Finally, let's
talk about hybrid clouds.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:01.040
If you're a company with a
lot of on-premise servers,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.160
chances are you're not too keen
to buy new racks every month

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.080
to handle spikes in workloads.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.220
Instead, GCP offers a
reliable VPN service

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.650
that allows on
demand GCP instances

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:15.434
to connect securely to
your on-premise systems.

00:04:15.434 --> 00:04:16.850
That means that
you can seamlessly

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:19.550
spin up new instances
in Google's cloud

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:22.490
to handle workload
spikes and spin them down

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:23.550
when they aren't needed--

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.360
which is way better than buying
them yourself and having them

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.650
sit around idle when
they're not being used.

00:04:28.650 --> 00:04:31.860
So if you want more recaps
on great Next content,

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.860
make sure to check out
the rest of our playlist.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.020
And don't forget
the next world tour,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.430
coming soon to a city near you.

